Overview
The core philosophy is that uncommon wealth is generated not by trading market cycles, but by identifying companies with massive runways for compounding growth, exceptional management, and a durable competitive edge—and holding them for years or decades.

Mentor's Insight: After 20+ years, I can tell you that this philosophy is simple to understand but brutally difficult to execute. The challenge isn't intellectual—it's emotional. You will watch your carefully researched companies drop 30%, 40%, even 50% during market panics. You will hear "experts" tell you you're wrong. Fisher's framework gives you the conviction to hold. Without it, you'll sell at the worst possible moment.

The 15 Points to Look for in a Common Stock
Before initiating a position, a company should pass the vast majority of these qualitative filters:

Point	Question	Why It Matters
1	Market Potential: Does the company have products or services with sufficient market potential to make possible a sizable increase in sales for at least several years?	Without growth runway, even great management can't deliver returns
2	Future Innovation: Does management have a determination to continue to develop products or processes that will further increase sales potentials when current attractive product lines are largely exploited?	Complacency kills; you need continuous innovation
3	R&D Effectiveness: How effective are the company's research and development efforts in relation to its size?	Small companies can out-innovate giants if focused
4	Sales Organization: Does the company have an above-average sales organization?	Great products don't sell themselves
5	Profit Margins: Does the company have a worthwhile profit margin?	Without margins, growth is meaningless
6	Margin Improvement: What is the company doing to maintain or improve profit margins?	Margin erosion is a death spiral
7	Labor Relations: Does the company have outstanding labor and personnel relations?	People create value; unhappy workers destroy it
8	Executive Relations: Does the company have outstanding executive relations?	Top talent must want to stay
9	Management Depth: Does the company have depth to its management?	What happens when the founder leaves?
10	Accounting Controls: How good are the company's cost analysis and accounting controls?	You can't manage what you can't measure
11	Industry Clues: Are there other aspects of the business, peculiar to the industry, that give important clues about how outstanding the company may be relative to competition?	Every industry has unique success factors
12	Long-Range Outlook: Does the company have a short-range or long-range outlook in regard to profits?	Short-term focus leads to long-term failure
13	Equity Dilution: In the foreseeable future, will growth require sufficient equity financing so that new shares largely cancel existing stockholders' benefit from this anticipated growth?	Growth funded by share issuance can leave you with nothing
14	Management Candor: Does management talk freely when things are going well but "clam up" when troubles occur?	Transparency under pressure reveals character
15	Unquestionable Integrity: Does the company have a management of unquestionable integrity?	The non-negotiable point—without it, nothing else matters
Mentor's Insight: Point 15 is the gatekeeper. I've violated this rule three times in my career, chasing what looked like exceptional growth. Each time, I lost money. Each time, the warning signs were there—I just chose to ignore them. If integrity is absent, walk away. There are always other companies.

Agent Implementation ODC:

Print this checklist and use it for every major investment consideration

Score each point 1-5 (5 = exceptional)

Any point scored 1 is an automatic red flag requiring deep investigation

Point 15 must score 5, or the process stops immediately

No company should proceed without scoring at least 4 on points 1, 2, 5, 9, and 14

Core Learnings by Chapter
Chapter 1: Clues from the Past
Original Summary:

Hold for the long term: Wealth is built by holding exceptional companies through market cycles

The power of reinvestment: Target companies that institutionalize innovation and aggressively reinvest cash flows

Expanded Insights:

Stocks outperform bonds over the long term—even economic downturns create only temporary effects for quality businesses

Growth potential matters more than current size. Don't be seduced by large, stagnant companies simply because they're "safe"

Inflation makes stocks the only logical long-term choice. Outstanding businesses raise prices and grow through it

Mentor's Takeaway: I've watched countless investors lose fortunes trying to time markets. Fisher's insight here is simple but profound: your energy is better spent finding great companies than predicting what the Federal Reserve will do next month.

Agent Implementation ODC:

When evaluating a company, prioritize future growth runway over current size

Ask: "Can this company grow for the next 5-10 years regardless of economic conditions?"

Reject the temptation to time entries based on macroeconomic forecasts

Chapter 2: What "Scuttlebutt" Can Do
Original Summary:

Go beyond financials: Annual reports are polished PR. Seek ground truth

360-degree ecosystem: Talk to competitors, suppliers, customers, and former employees

Expanded Insights:

The best information isn't in financial statements. Annual reports are polished, sanitized, and often misleading

Talk to everyone connected to the business:

Competitors (they'll tell you where the company is weak and strong)

Vendors and suppliers (they see order patterns and payment reliability)

Customers (they experience the products and service firsthand)

Former employees (they reveal cultural and management truths)

Trade associations and industry experts

Outstanding companies stand out clearly through this research, even to moderately experienced investors

Mentor's Takeaway: In my early years, I skipped this step—too busy, too confident in my spreadsheet skills. I paid for it with losses that could have been avoided. Today, I won't touch a company until I've spoken with at least five people in its ecosystem.

Agent Implementation ODC:

Before any investment, conduct minimum 5 scuttlebutt interviews

Document findings from each source type: competitor, supplier, customer, former employee, industry expert

Look for patterns—if 3 of 5 sources mention the same strength or weakness, it's real

Compare what you hear against management's public statements

If sources consistently contradict management narratives, consider it a red flag

