Chapter 4: What to Buy: Applying Needs to Your Desires
Original Summary:

Match risk to temperament: Align portfolio with psychological tolerance

Differentiate between large, conservative compounders and highly volatile, aggressive innovators

Expanded Insights:

If you lack time or skill, find the right advisor. But choose carefully:

Ensure their fundamental philosophy matches yours

Verify their track record comes from genuine skill, not luck or excessive risk

Confirm their integrity is unquestionable

Growth stocks come in two sizes:

Large, conservative growth stocks: Temporary losses possible but reward handsomely over time; typically pay dividends

Smaller growth companies: Potentially more profitable but higher risk; typically reinvest all earnings

Fisher personally prefers low-dividend growth companies (reinvested capital creates more value), but acknowledges income needs may dictate otherwise

Mentor's Takeaway: Be honest with yourself about your time, temperament, and skill. There's no shame in using a good advisor—I know brilliant doctors and lawyers who do exactly that. But most "advisors" are salespeople in disguise. Do your due diligence on them like you would on a stock.

Agent Implementation ODC:

Honestly assess: Do I have 10+ hours per week for research? If no, consider indexing

If using an advisor, conduct scuttlebutt on them—talk to current clients, check regulatory history

Match company size to your risk tolerance: large caps for stability, small caps for growth, but never exceed your sleep-well threshold

Document your personal risk tolerance in writing before any investment

Chapter 5: When to Buy
Original Summary:

Ignore macro forecasting: Don't try to time the broader economy

Buy the "hiccup": Enter when a fundamentally sound company faces a temporary, solvable operational snag

Expanded Insights:

The best buying opportunities come from temporary difficulties:

Plants operating below capacity

Short-term expenses eating into profits

New product launch delays

Industry-specific setbacks

Investigate thoroughly to ensure problems are truly temporary. Permanent deterioration isn't a buying opportunity—it's a value trap

Ignore general market levels (except in extreme cases like impending depressions)

If you're new or uncertain, dollar-cost average over several years

Mentor's Takeaway: The greatest purchases of my career happened during company-specific panics. When everyone else sees disaster, I see Fisher's "temporary problem." But I only trust that judgment after doing the scuttlebutt work to confirm it's truly temporary.

Agent Implementation ODC:

Maintain a watchlist of companies that passed the 15-point screen

When any watchlist company drops 20%+ on company-specific news, investigate immediately

Ask: "Is this problem temporary or permanent?" Use scuttlebutt to verify

If temporary, buy aggressively. If permanent, remove from watchlist

Never buy solely because "the market is down" — macro declines are not buying signals without company-specific research

Chapter 6: When to Sell—And When Not To
Original Summary:

The rule: If the job was done correctly, the time to sell is almost never

Valid exits: Sell only if you made a factual error, the fundamental thesis is broken, or to fund a spectacularly better opportunity

Expanded Insights:

Selling Reason	Description
1. You made a mistake	The original purchase thesis was wrong; cut losses and move on
2. The company changed	Management deteriorated or future prospects no longer justify holding
3. You found something better	Rare, given how difficult it is to find truly outstanding companies
Invalid Selling Arguments (Rejected by Fisher):

"The market is going to decline." If you can't time purchases based on macro, you can't time sales either

"The stock is overvalued (high P/E)." Superior businesses deserve superior multiples due to higher expected growth

"The price has surged." Past price movement is irrelevant to current value

Mentor's Takeaway: My biggest investing mistakes haven't been buying wrong—they've been selling right. I've sold compounders too early more times than I can count, always for one of those "invalid" reasons. Learn from my pain: if the company remains outstanding, hold on.

Agent Implementation ODC:

Before any sale, write down your reason and classify it: Mistake, Company Changed, or Better Opportunity

If reason is "market concerns" or "valuation seems high" or "I've made enough profit" — DO NOT SELL

When considering "Better Opportunity," compare both companies against the 15-point checklist. Only sell if the new company scores significantly higher

Review holdings annually against original thesis. If thesis still holds, hold. If thesis broken, sell regardless of price

Chapter 7: The Hullabaloo About Dividends
Original Summary:

Tax inefficiency: Dividends drag down returns due to double taxation

Retained earnings win: Greatest compounding occurs when cash is reinvested internally at high rates

Expanded Insights:

Dividends aren't inherently good or bad. The critical factor is whether the company can reinvest earnings at high rates of return

If a company generates 20% returns on reinvested capital, shareholders are better off with no dividends

Mature companies with limited reinvestment opportunities should pay dividends. But growing companies with abundant opportunities should retain earnings

Don't let dividend yield drive purchase decisions

Mentor's Takeaway: I've watched investors avoid phenomenal growth companies because they paid tiny dividends—then watched those companies compound at 15-20% annually for decades. The dividend yield on your original cost becomes irrelevant when the stock is up 50x.

Agent Implementation ODC:

When evaluating dividend policy, ask: "Can this company reinvest cash at >15% returns?"

If yes, prefer no dividend or minimal dividend

If no, dividend is appropriate and expected

Never screen for "high dividend yield" as a primary criterion

View share buybacks at reasonable prices as tax-efficient dividend alternatives

Chapter 8: Five Don'ts for Investors
Original Summary:

Don't buy promotional (unproven) companies

Don't ignore a great stock just because it trades Over-The-Counter (OTC)

Don't buy purely based on optimistic annual reports

Don't assume high P/E means growth is already priced in

Don't quibble over pennies—use market orders for exceptional companies

Expanded Insights:
Fisher's "don'ts" are practical warnings against common investor mistakes. Each addresses a specific behavioral trap.

Mentor's Takeaway: Number four—over-diversification—is perhaps the most misunderstood concept in modern investing. Index funds are fine for most people, but if you're doing active investing, you can't own 50 stocks and expect to outperform.

Agent Implementation ODC:

Create a "Don't List" and review before each purchase

When considering a stock, explicitly check against each don't

For promotional companies: require 3 years of operating history and proven revenue

For OTC stocks: conduct extra scuttlebutt (less information available)

For annual reports: verify claims through independent sources before trusting

For high P/E: compare to growth rate—PEG under 1.5 may still be reasonable

For price quibbling: if it's a 15-point company, buy at market and don't look back

