Chapter 10: How I Go About Finding a Growth Stock
Original Summary:

The funnel: Build systematic network of industry experts and efficient screens

Management last: Perform scuttlebutt first, only speak to management at the end to test integrity

Expanded Insights:
Fisher shares his personal process for discovering investment opportunities:

Start with scuttlebutt, not screens. Talk to people in industries you understand

Look for companies where something is changing—new management, new products, new market opportunities

Develop a chain of information. One knowledgeable contact leads to another

Be patient. Finding great companies takes time

Mentor's Takeaway: Fisher's approach is the opposite of today's screen-and-filter mentality. He starts with people, not numbers. I've adopted this: my best ideas have come from suppliers, competitors, and customers long before they appeared on any financial radar.

Agent Implementation ODC:

Build a contact network across 5-10 industries you understand

Schedule 2-3 scuttlebutt conversations per month, even when not looking for ideas

When a contact mentions a company positively multiple times, add to watchlist

Only contact management after completing external research

When speaking to management, focus on testing integrity and clarifying unknowns—not getting "the story"

Chapter 11: Summary and Conclusion
Original Summary:

Discipline is key: A rigorous framework is useless without the psychological fortitude to hold during drawdowns

Skill compounds over decades; luck does not

Expanded Insights:
Fisher reiterates his core philosophy: investing is about finding exceptional businesses and holding them through market cycles. The work is in the research; the reward comes from patience and compounding.

Mentor's Takeaway: After twenty-plus years in this business, I can tell you with confidence: Philip Fisher's approach works. It's not easy—the scuttlebutt method requires effort, the fifteen points demand judgment, and holding through volatility tests your soul. But it works.

